Touches in the edge strips reach the app until they swipe (Omarchy Mobile)

As Android's gestures: a touch that lands in one of the shell's edge strips
(layers omarchy-mobile-back and omarchy-mobile-back-right, back from the
left and right edges; omarchy-mobile-navigation, home and the switcher from
the bottom) goes to the surface under it, so taps and scrolls at the edges
reach the app. Once it moves 12 logical pixels inward, and 1.2 times more
than across, the app gets wl_touch.cancel and the strip gets the touch, as
a touch down where the finger landed; its later events skip the event bus
(through it every motion after the takeover came back cancelled). 16 pixels
across first leave it to the app; a second finger ends the watch. Over a
surface of the strip's own client (the shell's wallpaper in an app's
margin) the strip takes the touch itself: Qt mixed up a touch cancelled in
one of its windows and pressed again in another. A cancel also resets the
seat's touch lock count, since it ends every touch point of the client.

